WEBVTT

00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:11.567
[handbrake releasing]

00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:13.413
[car starting]

00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:16.600
[engine revving]

00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:27.000
[Jerry]<i> This is a 1959 </i>
<i>Fiat Jolly in coral orange.</i>

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.832
<i>It has a two-cylinder,</i>
<i>22-horsepower engine</i>

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:36.833
<i>that can reach a speed of up to</i>
<i>65 miles per hour with no doors.</i>

00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:42.533
<i>It does have a rear body panel</i>
<i>in the event of an accident</i>

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.933
<i>to hold your kids inside</i>
<i>for an extra second</i>

00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:47.133
<i>before they are also hurled into oblivion.</i>

00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:52.167
<i>Only 100 to 200 Fiat Jollys were built,</i>

00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:55.400
<i>and believe it or not,</i>
<i>they are quite sought after these days.</i>

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.833
<i>The word "jolly" means "joker" in Italian.</i>

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.367
<i>It also means something light, fun, funny</i>
<i>and pretty in several other languages.</i>

00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:07.733
<i>"Insane" might have been a better name.</i>

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:11.367
<i>Even Italians have</i>
<i>varied opinions about it.</i>

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:13.440
[in Italian]
What kind of car is this? It's so small!

00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:15.000
But look how nice this is.

00:01:15.142 --> 00:01:16.543
Do you see what the seats are like?

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.212
It's not supposed to be a Cadillac!

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:22.449
In my opinion, this isn't a real car.
So what are you talking about?

00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:24.467
[in English] Where you want to go,
the Alps with this car?

00:01:24.518 --> 00:01:25.852
[in Italian] It's for the beach!

00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:29.133
If you like it, that's okay,
but I don't like it at all.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:31.625
Fine, then. Go take a walk.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.633
[Jerry] <i>This car's like a puppy</i>
<i>that sticks its nose under your hand</i>

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.400
<i>because it wants to be petted.</i>

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.167
<i>Hitler would love this car.</i>

00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:42.267
[phone ringing]

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.205
<i>-</i>[Louis] <i>Hello?</i>
<i>-Louis? It's Jerry.</i>

00:01:45.272 --> 00:01:46.807
[Louis] <i>Jerry, what's going on?</i>

00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:49.267
[Jerry] <i>Remember we talked about</i>
<i>going out on your boat?</i>

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.233
[Louis] <i>Yeah.</i>

00:01:50.277 --> 00:01:52.112
[Jerry] <i>Do you have a coffee machine</i>
<i>on your boat?</i>

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:54.381
[Louis] <i>I do. I can make you coffee</i>
<i>on the boat, sure.</i>

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.033
[Jerry] <i>Great. On my way.</i>

00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:02.067
[Jerry] <i>Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld</i>

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:05.367
<i>and this is</i>
<i></i>Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:07.533
<i>Today my guest is Louis C.K.</i>

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:11.600
<i>Louis is a fantastic stand-up comedian,</i>
<i>who also has his own show</i>

00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:15.733
<i>that he writes, stars and directs,</i>
<i>which is also called</i> Louie.

00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:19.867
<i>On today's show,</i>
<i>you're gonna see Louis do something</i>

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:22.476
<i>I'm sure you had no idea he could do.</i>

00:02:25.967 --> 00:02:26.967
[handbrake pulled]

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.600
[door opens]

00:02:30.967 --> 00:02:32.000
We all set?

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:34.054
You wouldn't be here
if you weren't all set.

00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:38.733
-[Jerry] So I got a car.
-[Louis] Oh, my God.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.500
-[Louis] What kind of engine is in there?
-It's a two-cylinder.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:42.767
It's a two-cylinder?

00:02:42.833 --> 00:02:44.500
-Like an old motorcycle.
-Yeah.

00:02:44.633 --> 00:02:46.000
One for me, one for you.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.300
[laughing]

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:50.200
[Jerry] Can you imagine walking into
a car dealership and going,

00:02:50.437 --> 00:02:52.139
-"Yeah, I'll take that."
-[Louis laughing]

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.667
How's it in the snow? How's the wicker?

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.800
-"How's the wicker in the snow?"
-In the snow?

00:02:56.867 --> 00:02:57.700
[car starting]

00:02:57.767 --> 00:02:59.333
-That's how you start it?
-Yeah.

00:02:59.533 --> 00:03:00.433
Wow.

00:03:00.814 --> 00:03:03.383
Okay, you're not gonna believe this,
but we're moving.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.533
That's amazing.

00:03:08.455 --> 00:03:10.490
[Louis] The guy on the bicycle
almost took you out.

00:03:13.367 --> 00:03:14.367
[honking]

00:03:14.733 --> 00:03:15.567
[honking back]

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.067
What a horn.

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:18.200
[tires screeching]

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.400
Now I feel like I'm in Italy.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.033
[Jerry] This must be pretty funny looking.

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:28.400
[Louis] From outside? Yeah.

00:03:28.442 --> 00:03:29.776
This is ridiculous.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.700
It might be the worst car ever
to be driven in Manhattan.

00:03:32.746 --> 00:03:35.048
I'd be hard-pressed to think
of something less appropriate.

00:03:35.115 --> 00:03:37.951
[Louis] I like that I could actually
drag my knuckles on the street.

00:03:38.018 --> 00:03:40.787
-[Jerry] You could touch the street.
-You could play polo.

00:03:40.854 --> 00:03:42.689
[honking]

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:45.158
-Really?
-[honking]

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.033
How can you get mad at this car?

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.467
It's funny, people are right there.
There's a bicyclist.

00:03:50.533 --> 00:03:52.633
My face is at his ass level.

00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:55.767
-Go ahead, give it a little squeeze.
-No, I'm afraid.

00:03:55.867 --> 00:03:57.367
[both laughing]

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:05.833
-[Louis] We've known each other for...
-[Jerry] Twenty years?

00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:09.700
[Louis] More than that. Because I opened
for you at Stitches in Boston.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.300
And I was, like, 19 years old.

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:15.300
-Do you consider yourself a New Yorker?
-New York is my home for sure.

00:04:15.533 --> 00:04:17.200
Boston is distant from me.

00:04:17.324 --> 00:04:20.360
And also I came to Boston from Mexico
when I was a kid.

00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:22.700
And what is it
you would like to know about me?

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:25.767
[laughing]

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.233
[Jerry] I don't know why people
always assume the guest

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.733
is more interesting than the host.

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:34.775
I desperately wanted to go
to New York University Film School...

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:36.009
-Really?
-and be a filmmaker.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.100
I wanted that desperately.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.500
But I couldn't fill out...

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.249
the form at my high school.

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:45.218
And I would get, like,
some food on it or something.

00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:46.800
And then I just would never do it.

00:04:50.067 --> 00:04:52.467
[Jerry] How many years you think
you'll do your show?

00:04:52.533 --> 00:04:54.800
I'd say seven or eight, tops.

00:04:54.933 --> 00:04:56.933
Why? Why would you ever stop?

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.600
You stopped after nine years.

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:00.400
Yeah, but your show's different.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.200
Your show can grow with you.

00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:04.933
My show was about four single people...

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.867
-Yeah. Right.
-...living this certain type of lifestyle.

00:05:07.933 --> 00:05:10.133
We didn't wanna do
Kramer's 50th birthday party.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:11.044
[laughing]

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:16.800
Do you think you'll
ever shave this thing off?

00:05:16.933 --> 00:05:18.867
I tried once. I did it for a movie.

00:05:18.933 --> 00:05:21.700
My chin just goes from here to my neck.

00:05:21.767 --> 00:05:23.400
Looks like a fine chin to me.

00:05:23.533 --> 00:05:24.667
Well, thank you.

00:05:24.933 --> 00:05:27.200
Most comedians do not have facial hair.

00:05:30.233 --> 00:05:32.867
-How do you-- What are you basing that on?
-It's very unusual.

00:05:32.933 --> 00:05:35.533
Have you thought that before you just
said it? Or did you just...

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.500
I like the story
of Chris Rock going on <i>SNL.</i>

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.433
Him telling Lorne,
"I wanna keep my mustache and goatee."

00:05:42.833 --> 00:05:46.600
And Lorne said to him,
"In comedy, we put on beards."

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:49.133
[both laughing]

00:05:53.133 --> 00:05:57.867
If we got hit by an SUV,
we would just be smears.

00:05:57.933 --> 00:05:59.667
-Yeah.
-We would just be

00:05:59.867 --> 00:06:02.400
pink and red smears
with teeth stuck in it.

00:06:02.529 --> 00:06:03.496
And a Nike logo.

00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:05.732
Would anybody do
a roadside memorial for us?

00:06:05.799 --> 00:06:07.067
I don't think anybody would.

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:10.170
[Jerry] Have you noticed the white painted
bicycles? That's what those are.

00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:12.333
That's a person that died on a bike?

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.467
-Oh, jeez.
-I know, it's like, "Come on.

00:06:15.633 --> 00:06:22.000
Must we all get bummed
every day back and forth to work?"

00:06:22.082 --> 00:06:23.583
It sometimes doesn't work out.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.318
[laughing]
Sometimes, it doesn't work out.

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:32.626
Is this your boat?

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:34.394
-[bleep] No.
-[Jerry] That's the <i>Utopia 3.</i>

00:06:34.467 --> 00:06:36.300
-[Louis] <i>Utopia 3.</i>
-[Jerry] That the worst sign

00:06:36.363 --> 00:06:38.398
-you've seen on the side of a boat?
-[Louis] The ugliest...

00:06:38.565 --> 00:06:40.734
-That's like a disco in Germany.
-Yeah. [laughing]

00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:43.000
People with boats
usually have very bad taste.

00:06:43.233 --> 00:06:46.300
-My ass hurts a lot.
-From the wicker?

00:06:47.633 --> 00:06:48.567
I hope so.

00:06:48.833 --> 00:06:50.033
[Louis laughing]

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:57.867
How would your life be different

00:06:57.933 --> 00:07:00.967
if you had a 100 times the amount
of money that you have?

00:07:01.233 --> 00:07:04.200
I'd probably use it to make stuff.
I'd probably have...

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:06.760
You know, I'd make movies
with my own money probably.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.967
Max Bialystock said,
"Never put your money in the show."

00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:12.033
Yeah, well, he's not a great role model.

00:07:17.833 --> 00:07:20.467
[Louis] I'm amazed
at this massive structure.

00:07:20.533 --> 00:07:23.667
It's the most prominent structure
on the river of Manhattan.

00:07:23.733 --> 00:07:26.833
And it catches one golf ball like this.
That's all it does.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.100
[Jerry] We're not gonna
fill up the river with balls.

00:07:29.167 --> 00:07:30.000
[Louis] No.

00:07:30.067 --> 00:07:32.433
Wouldn't that be funny if it topped out,
filled with golf balls?

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:33.333
-The whole river.
-Yeah.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:37.267
We could just walk to New Jersey
on golf balls.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.433
Or you put them over in Jersey,
and hit them back.

00:07:39.533 --> 00:07:41.100
[Louis laughing]

00:07:41.567 --> 00:07:43.467
-Larry played golf with the president.
-He did.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:45.967
Here's how my relationship works
with Larry David.

00:07:46.033 --> 00:07:48.233
We were on the phone for a half hour,
two days ago,

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:49.633
and it didn't come up.

00:07:50.267 --> 00:07:51.867
[laughing]

00:07:52.467 --> 00:07:54.233
Where we going? We went the wrong way?

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.100
No, I just think you would look great
in that shirt.

00:07:57.167 --> 00:08:01.133
-The colors, with your hair.
-I don't look good in orange.

00:08:01.433 --> 00:08:03.400
Really? You are orange.

00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:06.500
I know, that's why. It's too much.
Too much of a good thing.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:10.567
[Louis] This guy Alex,
he lives on his boat.

00:08:10.633 --> 00:08:13.200
He helps me with my boat and stuff.
He's like my boat guy.

00:08:17.467 --> 00:08:20.467
[Jerry] Wow! That is handsome.
You know what it looks like?

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:23.667
-It looks like the orca from <i>Jaws.</i>
-[Louis] I always wanted the orca.

00:08:23.767 --> 00:08:25.433
Do you ever lean over the side and go,

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.333
"That's a 20-footer."
And you go, "Twenty-five."

00:08:27.407 --> 00:08:28.541
"Twenty-five."

00:08:28.767 --> 00:08:31.400
I don't think suede is the right fabric
for boating.

00:08:31.467 --> 00:08:32.567
No, it really isn't.

00:08:35.133 --> 00:08:36.333
[Louis] See these things?

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:38.033
These are fenders.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:41.633
Once we're past the <i>Queen Mary</i> thing here,
you gotta pull all the fenders up

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.900
and just lay them.
That's all you gotta do.

00:08:43.967 --> 00:08:45.200
Aye, me hearties.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:46.833
Okay, here go the engines.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.000
[engine starts]

00:08:48.133 --> 00:08:49.700
[engine revving]

00:08:51.433 --> 00:08:54.233
Comedy eyeball, don't forget that.
You need that?

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:55.802
-No, that stays in here.
-Okay.

00:08:55.869 --> 00:08:58.271
Yeah, that's how I can see
what's going on down here.

00:08:58.338 --> 00:08:59.239
Wow.

00:08:59.333 --> 00:09:02.333
-This is the flybridge.
-This does not look easy.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.467
Whenever I'm with my kids
and I do this part of coming out of here,

00:09:05.633 --> 00:09:07.733
-I'm always yelling at them to shut up.
-Right.

00:09:07.833 --> 00:09:11.000
'Cause if they start chatting, I'm like,
"Shut up. You're really making this hard!

00:09:11.067 --> 00:09:13.200
-I love you. Stop it."
-[Jerry laughs]

00:09:13.733 --> 00:09:17.267
[Jerry] I think you like boats
because you look good on a boat.

00:09:17.333 --> 00:09:19.700
-Look at you, you're Quint.
-[Louis] I am Quint.

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:20.927
[Jerry laughs]

00:09:21.433 --> 00:09:22.333
[engine revving]

00:09:24.197 --> 00:09:25.098
Backing up.

00:09:29.903 --> 00:09:32.305
[Jerry] Your eyes are darting around
like you're a fugitive.

00:09:32.372 --> 00:09:33.540
A lot of it is breathing.

00:09:34.233 --> 00:09:35.367
[exhales and laughs]

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.433
[Louis] This is a mother [bleep] today.

00:09:38.667 --> 00:09:40.833
Pulling me right towards
all that rotting wood.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.533
-Why don't they get rid of that?
-It would be really hard.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.133
Like pulling teeth out of a serpent's head
or something. I don't know.

00:09:47.233 --> 00:09:48.667
[Jerry] Is there gambling on that boat?

00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:51.124
[Louis] I think you're gambling
every time you go out on it.

00:09:51.467 --> 00:09:53.967
[Jerry] Wow. I can't believe
how well you did that.

00:09:54.067 --> 00:09:55.800
-[Louis] Thank you.
-That was masterful.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:59.267
All right, so now you need to go
get the fenders up.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.733
Okay. Now I'm Richard Dreyfuss.

00:10:02.233 --> 00:10:05.367
[Louis] "It proves that a college boy
don't know when to admit when he's wrong."

00:10:05.533 --> 00:10:07.867
-I lost the fenders.
-You lost them?

00:10:07.933 --> 00:10:10.067
I was playing with the comedy eyeball.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.533
[chuckling]

00:10:11.778 --> 00:10:13.713
-They're gone? It's all right.
-They're gone.

00:10:16.567 --> 00:10:19.767
Now we're doing 16 knots,
which is great on the river.

00:10:20.633 --> 00:10:23.100
-[Jerry] You're having a blast now?
-[Louis] Very happy.

00:10:23.533 --> 00:10:24.367
[Jerry] Why?

00:10:24.433 --> 00:10:26.200
[Louis]
I didn't used to be able to do this.

00:10:26.267 --> 00:10:29.033
But it's amazing to me
that you can just take a boat...

00:10:30.533 --> 00:10:33.033
And over time,
figure out all this [bleep] .

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.900
And the more things you can do,
you can go anywhere.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.600
I love that, and I love
coming out on the river.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:42.967
I'm the only person out here.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:44.600
[jazz music playing]

00:10:49.382 --> 00:10:51.451
-You wanna do all of them?
-No, just do the sides.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:53.900
Like your kid's jacket in the winter.

00:10:53.967 --> 00:10:56.500
Yeah, upside down at 20 knots.

00:10:56.567 --> 00:10:58.200
[laughing] Upside down.

00:11:01.167 --> 00:11:03.767
[Jerry] How many shows have you got
figured out so far?

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.900
Ten of them, I've finished writing.

00:11:05.967 --> 00:11:08.667
And I took on a couple
of new people to try.

00:11:08.733 --> 00:11:12.733
They sit on my couch while I write,
and it's somebody to talk to.

00:11:12.867 --> 00:11:14.500
And this time I used Steven Wright.

00:11:14.933 --> 00:11:15.800
Oh, yeah?

00:11:15.867 --> 00:11:19.267
When I grew up in Boston,
when I started doing stand-up,

00:11:19.333 --> 00:11:21.300
-he was everybody's hero.
-Sure.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:25.100
So you wanted to humiliate him
by having him work for you now,

00:11:25.167 --> 00:11:26.200
on a couch.

00:11:31.667 --> 00:11:35.067
[Jerry] Steven and I did a gig in the '80s
at a college football stadium,

00:11:35.133 --> 00:11:38.200
called Gator Growl, 80,000 people.

00:11:38.398 --> 00:11:40.466
George Burns had done it the year before.

00:11:40.533 --> 00:11:43.570
-What did George Burns do at Gator Growl?
-Twenty minutes.

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:46.172
But Steven had a great line.
They were asking us,

00:11:46.267 --> 00:11:49.433
"What's it like as a comedian
in front of 80,000 people?"

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.000
-Right.
-And Steven said,

00:11:51.467 --> 00:11:53.167
"If you're swimming in the ocean,

00:11:53.233 --> 00:11:57.233
it doesn't matter how deep the water is.
All you can do is swim."

00:11:57.417 --> 00:11:59.052
[laughs]

00:12:01.933 --> 00:12:04.333
I get really weirded out
going under big bridges.

00:12:13.633 --> 00:12:17.300
They have this commercial now
for this drug called Chantix.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:19.533
And they list all the side effects,

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:21.800
which is the worst comedy premise,
of course.

00:12:22.033 --> 00:12:24.433
-Yeah, of course.
-But I've never heard another one

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:27.200
where they say,
"You may experience weird dreams."

00:12:27.467 --> 00:12:29.200
-"Weird dreams."
-Weird dreams.

00:12:29.367 --> 00:12:32.100
-What dreams aren't weird?
-What dreams aren't weird?

00:12:32.167 --> 00:12:35.800
So I kind of imagine they have like,
a little phone bank set up at Chantix

00:12:35.967 --> 00:12:38.900
-with people calling in with their dreams.
-Right.

00:12:38.967 --> 00:12:40.400
"Bob, you think that one's weird?

00:12:40.467 --> 00:12:43.167
I got a guy here with orangutan feet."

00:12:43.233 --> 00:12:44.533
"No, I've had that one."

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:45.700
[laughing]

00:12:45.933 --> 00:12:46.767
"It's not weird."

00:12:49.435 --> 00:12:51.571
[Louis] I think we should
maybe anchor over here.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:52.967
[Jerry] That sounds good.

00:12:53.973 --> 00:12:56.209
-I can't afford this boat.
-No?

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:57.800
-I shouldn't have gotten it.
-Come on!

00:12:57.967 --> 00:13:01.367
Well, you shouldn't buy a yacht
unless you can afford ten yachts.

00:13:01.433 --> 00:13:03.933
-There's some kind of formula like that.
-That's stupid.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:05.367
-Is it? All right, good.
-Yes.

00:13:05.433 --> 00:13:08.133
-When I hit the water, just go like this.
-Okay.

00:13:08.187 --> 00:13:12.392
If the chain starts going off to the side,
just go like this or like this.

00:13:12.492 --> 00:13:13.760
[anchor chain rattling]

00:13:21.233 --> 00:13:22.067
[anchor drop]

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:25.100
[Louis] I lost an anchor under here once.

00:13:25.533 --> 00:13:27.067
Maybe James Cameron will find it.

00:13:27.367 --> 00:13:28.767
-You want a coffee?
-Yeah.

00:13:31.044 --> 00:13:33.780
-[Louis] Cappuccino or espresso?
-Cappuccino.

00:13:34.033 --> 00:13:37.000
-You really like things, don't you?
-Yeah, I like machines.

00:13:37.067 --> 00:13:40.200
-Yeah.
-I like the things that make stuff happen.

00:13:40.567 --> 00:13:43.067
[Louis] I was with my kids
waiting for a school bus

00:13:43.133 --> 00:13:45.833
and this dog walked by
with his owner, on the leash.

00:13:45.967 --> 00:13:48.000
The owner walked by
with his dog on a leash.

00:13:48.067 --> 00:13:50.233
"The dog walked by
with an owner on the leash"?

00:13:50.296 --> 00:13:52.332
I said it backwards
and then tried to fix it.

00:13:52.433 --> 00:13:55.933
[Jerry] My favorite dog thing is when
your dog is walking through the room,

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:59.167
and you stop him,
he doesn't then continue on his way.

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:01.567
-Whatever you interrupted is gone.
-It's gone.

00:14:03.167 --> 00:14:06.167
[Louis] I go to the 3D IMAX movies stoned.

00:14:06.212 --> 00:14:09.182
-[Jerry] Stoned or you get a scone?
-[giggling]

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:12.667
Yes, I get a scone to see a 3D IMAX movie.

00:14:12.833 --> 00:14:14.433
-You're so elegant.
-Yeah.

00:14:15.067 --> 00:14:17.267
If my kids have been with me for a stretch

00:14:17.467 --> 00:14:20.767
and then they go with their mom
and I have a day between them

00:14:20.833 --> 00:14:22.733
and some work to do or something,

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.067
I smoke some pot and see a 3D IMAX movie.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:27.367
Bring your own snacks
or do you get movie crap?

00:14:27.467 --> 00:14:30.700
I don't get the movie crap
because I don't want to buy it stoned.

00:14:30.867 --> 00:14:33.767
To me, talking to a stranger
when I'm stoned is like a nightmare.

00:14:33.833 --> 00:14:35.667
Why, because you can't read the signals?

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.567
Yeah, I just can't, it's all wrong,

00:14:37.633 --> 00:14:39.767
and they're looking at me
like I'm crazy, I think.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:42.933
And what's worse is if people recognize me

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:45.500
for being a known person

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.733
and they come over, "Oh, my God!"
And I'm really stoned.

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.100
So why do you go
to a crowded movie theater?

00:14:51.224 --> 00:14:52.925
[laughs] I have it down to a science.

00:14:53.133 --> 00:14:54.833
First, I buy the tickets online.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:57.967
I print it out, and I put it
in the same pocket every time,

00:14:58.133 --> 00:15:00.967
so I'm not doing this
"Stoned guy can't find his thing."

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:03.000
This pocket has no other function.

00:15:03.167 --> 00:15:05.900
-It's got that ticket in it.
-One ticket for one guy.

00:15:06.333 --> 00:15:07.633
One ticket for one guy.

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:09.367
I get some candy bars and treats.

00:15:09.567 --> 00:15:10.967
Like, an ice cream bar is good.

00:15:11.033 --> 00:15:14.433
You put that in one pocket,
maybe a Mounds bar or Snickers.

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:16.033
You have to wear a jacket
to do this right.

00:15:16.167 --> 00:15:18.533
-It's like a suicide bomber.
-[chuckles] Yes.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:22.300
-The detonator's in here.
-It's the same preparation.

00:15:22.767 --> 00:15:24.833
I just get Uber,
the car service, to get me.

00:15:24.933 --> 00:15:27.133
I like to lower the window about halfway

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:29.833
and just look at people
on the streets of Manhattan.

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:32.933
When you're stoned,
that's an amazing thing to look at.

00:15:33.067 --> 00:15:35.967
People carrying their bags of stuff,
and just seeing them and going,

00:15:36.033 --> 00:15:39.533
"We're just worms,
and we're just carrying our worm meal."

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:42.100
And I go in and give
the lady my paper ticket.

00:15:42.175 --> 00:15:44.577
And I put on the crazy glasses,
and I sit here.

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:48.467
And then the great thing,
this is all I'm at it for.

00:15:48.533 --> 00:15:52.133
They show trailers regular size,
and you get lost in that.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.100
And then when it's time for the movie,
that screen grows...

00:15:55.367 --> 00:15:58.033
-Yeah.
-...and they have this 3D IMAX countdown.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:01.033
Where the numbers come at you
and envelop you.

00:16:01.467 --> 00:16:06.200
And it's all blue and crystal,
and it drives me crazy.

00:16:07.033 --> 00:16:09.600
It's my favorite thing in life.

00:16:09.867 --> 00:16:12.767
It's like sex, comedy when it's good...

00:16:12.905 --> 00:16:14.173
-Sex.
-Yeah.

00:16:14.307 --> 00:16:15.441
-Comedy.
-Yeah.

00:16:15.675 --> 00:16:16.609
The blue numbers.

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:20.046
-Yeah, sex is starting to go a bit down.
-And sex is dropping.

00:16:20.133 --> 00:16:21.267
[giggling]

00:16:21.481 --> 00:16:22.682
-Yes.
-That's a shame.

00:16:22.767 --> 00:16:25.633
-When sex gets below the blue numbers...
-Yeah.

00:16:26.033 --> 00:16:28.500
-I have, you know...
-Pull the ripcord.

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:36.028
[Jerry] Know what the first thing
I said as a child,

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:37.700
before I said, "Mama" or "Dada"?

00:16:37.833 --> 00:16:39.100
"Go [bleep] yourself."

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:40.733
I said, "Leave me alone."

00:16:41.701 --> 00:16:42.935
[giggling]

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.733
That's amazing. My God.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:48.800
But is that not the mantra
of stand-up comedy?

00:16:48.867 --> 00:16:50.567
Leave me alone,

00:16:50.933 --> 00:16:51.833
okay?

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:53.967
You know, people talk about kids today.

00:16:54.313 --> 00:16:55.348
How they grow up too fast.

00:16:55.433 --> 00:16:59.733
I feel like I can only take a year
of each year. It can't...

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:00.933
[chuckling]

00:17:01.067 --> 00:17:04.500
"No more of you as a 9-year-old.
I've had it. Get me a 10-year-old."

00:17:04.567 --> 00:17:07.633
I don't know,
I sometimes miss the older versions...

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:09.133
-Sure, yeah.
-I look back.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:10.667
You can miss anything in life, Louis.

00:17:10.733 --> 00:17:12.167
Yeah, you can, even if it's bad.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.467
I had that thought this morning 'cause
my oldest is going to middle school.

00:17:15.535 --> 00:17:17.470
It's a real schlep now, the morning,

00:17:17.637 --> 00:17:19.939
and I thought,
"This is gonna be a tough winter."

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.533
Then my second thought was,
"I'm gonna miss this.

00:17:24.310 --> 00:17:26.679
There's gonna be a day
where I'm not gonna do this again."

00:17:26.879 --> 00:17:28.281
That's very rich, Louis.

00:17:28.767 --> 00:17:30.967
-That's good stuff, right?
-That's good stuff, man.

00:17:42.467 --> 00:17:45.333
There's the George Washington Bridge
shadow over us.

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:50.367
[Jerry] Scary bridge moment.

00:17:53.267 --> 00:17:55.600
Look how beautiful
Riverside Drive is up here.

00:17:55.667 --> 00:17:57.400
-I love those buildings.
-Yeah.

00:17:57.467 --> 00:17:58.933
New York makes me crazy.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.600
-I love New York City.
-Yeah.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:03.600
I love the different brick colors
and the water towers.

00:18:03.667 --> 00:18:04.500
Yeah.

00:18:04.567 --> 00:18:06.933
And then the trees
and then rocks right before the river.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:07.833
I know.

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:10.500
[Louis] I ran this boat aground
in Harlem once, in the mud.

00:18:10.833 --> 00:18:12.867
<i>It was the first time</i>
<i>my kids were on my boat.</i>

00:18:13.333 --> 00:18:15.500
<i>And I see this little cove, and I say,</i>

00:18:15.567 --> 00:18:19.267
<i>"Let's go in the cove,</i>
<i>and I'll make some macaroni and cheese."</i>

00:18:19.333 --> 00:18:22.033
<i>-I'm so happy to show my kids the boat.</i>
<i>-</i>[Jerry]<i> Right.</i>

00:18:22.101 --> 00:18:23.970
[Louis] <i>I'm also nervous</i>
<i>they're not gonna like it.</i>

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:26.533
<i>We run right into a </i>[bleep]<i> mud...</i>

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.067
<i>We just go right in the mud.</i>

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.433
<i>It was low tide,</i>
<i>I didn't read the chart correctly.</i>

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:34.000
<i>I'm in mud.</i>

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:37.200
<i>There's a park 30 feet away,</i>
<i>people barbecuing.</i>

00:18:37.267 --> 00:18:40.000
<i>And there's a park ranger</i>
<i>and he yells out,</i>

00:18:40.067 --> 00:18:43.067
<i>"You're stuck there. It's low tide.</i>
<i>You're stuck till, like, midnight."</i>

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:46.233
<i>My kids are really crying now.</i>
<i>I'm like, "Shut up," to the guy.</i>

00:18:46.367 --> 00:18:48.933
<i>And I get on the radio and I go,</i>
<i>"Coast guard."</i>

00:18:48.995 --> 00:18:50.863
<i>I thought the coast guard would come</i>
<i>dig me out.</i>

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:52.665
<i>-</i>[Jerry]<i> Yeah.</i>
<i>-But they didn't even answer.</i>

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:56.700
[Louis] <i>Now the water's receded so much</i>
<i>that there's mud all around me.</i>

00:18:56.967 --> 00:19:01.400
<i>The police boat is in the water</i>
<i>and the guy yells across,</i>

00:19:01.467 --> 00:19:03.567
<i>"Are your kids okay?"</i>
<i>And I go, "They're all right."</i>

00:19:03.733 --> 00:19:06.000
<i>He asks if I could get out</i>
<i>and walk on the mud.</i>

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:08.100
<i>I took a pole and I feel,</i>
<i>it's like quicksand.</i>

00:19:08.247 --> 00:19:10.182
<i>-</i>[Jerry] <i>Yeah.</i>
<i>-</i>[Louis]<i> Would've died in the mud.</i>

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:13.233
<i>People are starting to form at the park,</i>
<i>starting to figure out who I am.</i>

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:14.967
[Jerry laughing]

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:17.200
And the cops say, "Are your kids hungry?"

00:19:17.267 --> 00:19:18.600
I go, "Yeah, we don't have a lot of--"

00:19:18.667 --> 00:19:21.633
We can't run the generator,
so we can't cook the macaroni and cheese.

00:19:21.867 --> 00:19:24.467
<i>So they've got hard macaroni and water,</i>
<i>that's all we have.</i>

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.567
<i>So the cops left and went to Whole Foods,</i>

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:29.367
<i>and they got us a chicken</i>
<i>and all kinds of food.</i>

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:33.200
<i>But now there was a big expanse of mud</i>
<i>between me and them.</i>

00:19:33.433 --> 00:19:34.900
<i>I needed to throw them a line.</i>

00:19:35.267 --> 00:19:38.000
<i>I throw them the line</i>
<i>and it's not getting there.</i>

00:19:38.067 --> 00:19:40.100
<i>I pull it back and now I'm covered in mud.</i>

00:19:40.367 --> 00:19:44.800
<i>I put a pot on the line, and I'm</i>
<i>swinging it and trying to throw it.</i>

00:19:45.267 --> 00:19:47.967
<i>And people on the shore are going,</i>
<i>"You can do it, Louis."</i>

00:19:48.033 --> 00:19:50.300
All these Hispanic people from Harlem.

00:19:50.633 --> 00:19:53.367
<i>And then it would go in the mud</i>
<i>and they'd go, "You suck, man."</i>

00:19:53.433 --> 00:19:54.967
<i>They're all, "Boo!"</i>

00:19:55.967 --> 00:19:58.533
<i>And finally the cops see a fisherman</i>
<i>in a fishing boat.</i>

00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:02.033
<i>They turn on their lights, they pull him</i>
<i>over, and they get him on their boat,</i>

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:04.900
<i>and he casts me a line</i>
<i>with a weight on it.</i>

00:20:05.237 --> 00:20:08.441
<i>And I tied my rope to his line,</i>
<i>and he pulled my line in.</i>

00:20:08.708 --> 00:20:12.278
<i>And then they tied a big garbage bag,</i>
<i>with food in it, to the line,</i>

00:20:12.367 --> 00:20:13.667
<i>and I stood on the back of the boat.</i>

00:20:13.733 --> 00:20:16.767
<i>And it was the hardest thing I ever did,</i>
<i>physically, in my life.</i>

00:20:16.867 --> 00:20:20.233
<i>But I wanted to feed my kids.</i>
<i>I was so desperate to feed my kids.</i>

00:20:20.833 --> 00:20:24.267
<i>And I pull this and people are cheering.</i>
<i>It was insane.</i>

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:25.733
<i>My oldest daughter was like,</i>

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:28.500
<i>"Daddy, take all your clothes off</i>
<i>in the shower of the boat."</i>

00:20:28.567 --> 00:20:31.067
<i>She gave me water bottles</i>
<i>and I cleaned off.</i>

00:20:31.367 --> 00:20:33.667
<i>Then we ate,</i>
<i>and we were there till midnight.</i>

00:20:33.733 --> 00:20:36.133
<i>This started at one o'clock.</i>
<i>We were there for almost 12 hours.</i>

00:20:36.202 --> 00:20:38.804
At one point it started raining,
while I was just sitting, waiting.

00:20:38.933 --> 00:20:42.667
<i>It's dead quiet now,</i>
<i>and I hear, in the park, some kid go,</i>

00:20:43.133 --> 00:20:45.667
<i>"Louis C.K., it's raining now!"</i>

00:20:46.467 --> 00:20:48.767
<i>And then this guy</i>
<i>who lives on his boat, he came...</i>

00:20:48.967 --> 00:20:50.933
<i>-That's how I met him.</i>
<i>-</i>[Jerry] <i>You called?</i>

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.300
[Louis] <i>I called, said, "Do you have</i>
<i>anybody that can help me?"</i>

00:20:53.367 --> 00:20:56.600
<i>He was dropped in the water</i>
<i>by a motorboat. He's a river fish.</i>

00:20:57.089 --> 00:20:59.725
<i>So he just jumped in the river</i>
<i>and swam to me,</i>

00:20:59.992 --> 00:21:02.628
<i>crawled on the mud, stood on my swim deck,</i>

00:21:02.695 --> 00:21:06.665
<i>and took off his dirty clothes, got into</i>
<i>some clean clothes he had in a bag.</i>

00:21:07.032 --> 00:21:09.235
-And he helped me get out of there.
-How did he do it?

00:21:09.433 --> 00:21:10.833
[Louis] <i>He knows everything.</i>

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:13.400
<i>He had a sense when the boat</i>
<i>was starting to get some movement.</i>

00:21:13.472 --> 00:21:16.408
<i>So we used the bow thrusters</i>
<i>to pull it around.</i>

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:19.800
<i>And then when we got loose,</i>
<i>we just gunned it out of there.</i>

00:21:19.867 --> 00:21:21.900
<i>The next day,</i>
<i>our picture was in the paper.</i>

00:21:22.033 --> 00:21:25.267
Daily News, <i>a picture of me and my kids</i>
<i>on the boat, stuck in the mud.</i>

00:21:25.833 --> 00:21:27.133
<i>And I say to my oldest,</i>

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:29.500
<i>"We have to come out</i>
<i>on the boat again soon,</i>

00:21:29.555 --> 00:21:31.690
<i>-so you can have a good experience."</i>
<i>-</i>[Jerry]<i> Right.</i>

00:21:31.767 --> 00:21:33.667
[Louis] <i>She said,</i>
<i>"We had a good experience."</i>

00:21:33.733 --> 00:21:34.633
[Jerry laughing]

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:35.867
<i>She was so happy.</i>

00:21:36.167 --> 00:21:37.867
That is one of the great stories.

00:21:37.930 --> 00:21:40.366
-Boy. That'll make a man out of you.
-Yeah.

00:21:40.699 --> 00:21:42.334
[Louis] <i>Well, out of my daughters, anyway.</i>

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:43.600
[Jerry chuckling]

00:21:53.867 --> 00:21:57.733
You know, we both did that big fancy
Robin Hood benefit at the Javits Center.

00:21:57.800 --> 00:21:58.933
Yeah, I got mad at them.

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:00.733
-Elton John opened the show.
-Right.

00:22:01.267 --> 00:22:04.200
-And he played "Tiny Dancer."
-Yeah.

00:22:04.267 --> 00:22:05.767
And "Bennie and the Jets."

00:22:06.133 --> 00:22:10.267
And they were talking at their tables.
Nobody was even looking at him.

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:11.767
And so, when I went on I said,

00:22:11.833 --> 00:22:15.433
"Well, I'm very excited to perform for you
after you talked through Elton John

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:18.333
like he was a piano player
in the Hilton lounge."

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:20.533
With all due respect to Elton John,

00:22:20.633 --> 00:22:23.133
-why don't you tell us who Bennie is?
-Right.

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:25.300
You know, is this a drug story,

00:22:25.367 --> 00:22:27.367
-what is it?
-Is it actual jets?

00:22:27.433 --> 00:22:28.967
-Is it a gay story?
-Right.

00:22:29.033 --> 00:22:30.133
Is that a gay bar?

00:22:30.367 --> 00:22:31.800
Are those all penises?

00:22:31.884 --> 00:22:33.853
-I'm just saying...
-But penises don't have wings.

00:22:33.919 --> 00:22:35.321
[giggling]

00:22:37.967 --> 00:22:40.867
-They will, if it gets any worse.
-[laughing] "They will."

00:22:40.933 --> 00:22:41.767
[laughing]

00:22:42.033 --> 00:22:43.200
[Louis] You just see.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.000
[both laughing]

00:22:50.667 --> 00:22:52.567
-Here, do me a favor, will you?
-Sure.

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:55.067
[giggles] Open my door for me.

00:22:55.341 --> 00:22:56.876
I don't want people
to know where I live.

00:22:57.142 --> 00:22:59.578
-So just the top one.
-All right, Louis, I'll see you later.

00:23:00.633 --> 00:23:04.500
Just the top lock at first.
It goes to the left, till it clicks.

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:07.533
There you go.
And then this one towards the knob.

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:08.433
[keys clicks]

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:09.900
-There you go.
-All right, see you.

00:23:09.967 --> 00:23:11.333
-All right.
-You wanna come in?

00:23:11.433 --> 00:23:13.600
-Can I use the bathroom? Yeah. Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:23:14.093 --> 00:23:16.729
[Jerry] Hey, how do you know how
to do the locks to my house?